### 0.1 RELIABILITY CONTRACT (NON-NEGOTIABLE, APPLIES TO EVERY PHASE)

This is the minimum bar for "the app works". It overrides any conflicting detail elsewhere in this prompt and must be verified before any phase is declared done.

1. **Zero-config boot.** The app must start and be fully usable in the AI Studio preview with NO secrets set. In `APP_ENV=development`, every missing secret is generated once and persisted (Firestore document `system_config/dev_secrets`, server-only, never sent to a client, refused when `APP_ENV` is `staging` or `production`), so sessions survive restarts and hot reloads. Missing optional integrations (Stripe, Gemini, Upstash, Firebase Auth config) fall back to their simulated or disabled state with a visible, translated explanation. They never crash a route and never throw at import time. Read environment variables only through one Zod-validated `lib/env.ts` that returns typed defaults. Never use `process.env.X!` at module top level elsewhere.
2. **Switches that switch.** A change of language (§6.9), display currency (§6.10) or account (§10.1b) takes effect immediately on every visible element of the current page, without losing the cart or form state, and survives reloads and navigation. Each has its own automated test (§16).
3. **One source of truth per preference.** Locale and display currency live in cookies written only by the helper in `lib/cookies.ts` and read on the server through `lib/preferences.ts`. Client components receive them through props or context. No component keeps its own copy of language or currency in `useState`, `localStorage` or Zustand that can drift from the cookie.
4. **No hard-coded user-visible strings or currency symbols** anywhere: components, Server Actions, errors, e-mails, PDFs, toasts, `aria-label`s, `<title>`, metadata. Server code returns error codes (for example `AUTH_CODE_EXPIRED`) and the UI translates them.
5. **Build gates.** Before finishing each phase run `tsc --noEmit`, `next build`, the Vitest suite and `scripts/verify-app.ts`, and fix every failure. Fix every console error and warning (including React hydration warnings) on the routes of that phase. If a gate truly cannot pass, list it in `README.md` under "Known issues". Never ship something silently broken.
6. **Proxy and iframe safety.** The AI Studio preview runs behind a reverse proxy and inside an iframe. Derive the public origin from `x-forwarded-host` and `x-forwarded-proto` when `APP_BASE_URL` is unset. Set `serverActions.allowedOrigins` in `next.config` from `APP_BASE_URL`, `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` and, outside production, the preview host. The Origin check in §10.2 compares against the same list. A Server Action rejected by origin validation shows a translated "Reload the page and try again" message, never a raw error.
7. **Forms and async UI.** Every submit button is disabled while pending (no double submissions), every field shows a translated inline error, and every data view has the four states of §3.
8. **Caching.** Authenticated pages send `Cache-Control: private, no-store`. Public pages that depend on locale or currency read cookies and are dynamic, never statically prerendered with a fixed locale.

## 1\. PRODUCT CONCEPT

Kaonashi is a high-end Japanese and sushi-fusion restaurant. This platform is a **phygital activation engine**. BTL (Below-The-Line) marketing in the physical world (flyers, table tents, coasters, takeaway boxes, pop-up events, street teams, partner venues) sends guests through a QR scan into a contextual mobile experience that converts into orders and reservations. Every step is attributed and measured. Restaurant operations (menu, orders, KDS, loyalty, inventory) are the conversion destination and the data source.

**Core loop. Every feature must support it:**

Physical touchpoint → QR scan → signed redirect and attribution cookie → campaign landing page → lead capture, coupon or instant-win → menu and cart (coupon auto-applied) → checkout or reservation → live order tracking → loyalty points → re-order → everything measured in the BTL funnel.

**Design for these people:**

- **Mia, guest (28):** on her phone at a bar, just scanned a coaster. Wants a discount in under 20 seconds, with no app install and no forced sign-up.  
- **Kenji, loyalty member:** returns often, cares about tier, points and the secret menu.  
- **Marketing manager:** creates campaigns, QR codes and coupons, and needs to know which printed piece actually produced orders.  
- **Itamae / kitchen lead:** works the KDS and live stock with wet hands on a tablet.  
- **Reception:** validates coupons in person and watches active orders.  
- **Sysmaster:** owns configuration, users and security.

## 2\. TECH STACK

- **Framework:** Next.js 14+ App Router, TypeScript strict, React Server Components and Server Actions. Select the Next.js framework in AI Studio Settings.  
- **UI:** Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui (Radix), `lucide-react` via normal named imports (`import { ShoppingBag } from "lucide-react"`). Do NOT deep-import `lucide-react/dist/...` paths.  
- **Data:** Cloud Firestore accessed server-side only, through repositories in `lib/data/*`. Clients never talk to Firestore directly. Firestore Security Rules deny all client access. If the platform's Firebase integration provisions the client SDK instead, keep the same repository layer and mirror the RBAC matrix (§9.6) in Security Rules: deny by default, custom claims `role` and `branchIds`. Every document has `branchId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`.  
- **Auth:** custom session layer.  
  - Staff: username and password (Argon2id via `@node-rs/argon2` (prebuilt binaries), then `argon2`, and as a last resort Node `crypto.scrypt` with N=2^15, r=8, p=1 and a per-user salt; record the active implementation in `docs/PLATFORM_ADAPTATIONS.md`) plus TOTP (`otplib`).  
  - Customers: guest checkout always works. Optional accounts through **Google sign-in** (Firebase Authentication Google provider, verified server-side) or an **e-mail one-time code** (6 digits, 10-minute expiry, stored hashed with a pepper, 5 tries maximum). Full rules, including account switching, are in §10.1b and override any simpler description.  
- **Sessions:** signed JWT (`jose`, HS256, 64-byte `SESSION_SECRET`, `kid` rotation) in cookie `__Host-kns_session` (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict, Path=/), backed by a server-side session record so sessions can be revoked. Every cookie the app sets is produced by the single helper described in §10.2; never set cookies ad hoc.  
- **AI:** Vercel AI SDK (`ai`, `@ai-sdk/react`, `@ai-sdk/google`) calling Gemini server-side only with `GEMINI_API_KEY`. The model name comes from env `GEMINI_MODEL` (default: the current Gemini Flash-class model available to the app). The key never reaches the browser.  
- **Payments:** Stripe in test mode (Payment Element, PaymentIntents, signed webhooks) behind a `PaymentProvider` adapter. With no Stripe keys, fall back to "Pay at venue / on delivery" and a deposit flow labelled "SIMULATED".  
- **Spreadsheets:** `exceljs` for .xlsx and `papaparse` for CSV. Do NOT install `xlsx` from the npm registry: it is stale (0.18.x) with known security advisories.  
- **QR and print:** `qrcode` (SVG and PNG) and `pdf-lib` for print sheets.  
- **Charts:** Recharts, themed through CSS variables.  
- **Validation and forms:** Zod everywhere, react-hook-form with the Zod resolver. Rich text (only landing-page copy) goes through `isomorphic-dompurify`. Never use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` with unsanitized content.  
- **Other:** `next-intl` with a cookie-based locale and NO locale prefix in URLs (§6.9), Zustand only for cart and KDS client state, Vitest for pricing, money, i18n, auth-flow and security logic. `firebase` (client SDK, used ONLY for Google sign-in, never for Firestore) and `firebase-admin` (server, to verify ID tokens).
- **Realtime:** Server-Sent Events from Node-runtime route handlers (`/api/stream/*`) with 3-second polling fallback.

